  "query_preview": "..(Tuesday through Sunday)\nIssued at 329 PM EDT SUN OCT 18 2026\n\nThe 18/12Z model suite analysis beginning Tuesday morning shows \ngood agreement through the upcoming work week. Broad upper-level \nridging will be situated over the heart of the CONUS with its \naxis initially roughly aligned with the Mississippi River. This\nupper level feature will continue to support robust surface high \npressure sprawling across much of eastern North America, centered \naround an ~1035 mb high south of James Bay. North to northeasterly\nlow-level flow will continue to filter down the Appalachians \nalong the eastern side of the high through the new work week,\nkeeping a dry, cool air mass in place over eastern Kentucky. \nUncertainty increases next weekend regarding the persistence of\nthe ridge as an upper-level trough may eject eastward from the \nPacific and lead to its break down. Even so, a slim majority of \nthe 00Z LREF members still favor the ridge holding firm into the \nlast week of October.\n\nIn sensible terms, the persistent surface ridging and northerly \nto easterly flow will keep fair and seasonably cool weather across\nall of eastern Kentucky with only a slow moderating trend. Highs \non Tuesday are forecast to range from the mid 60s to lower 70s, \nmoderating into the upper 60s to mid 70s by next Sunday. Meanwhile,\nnighttime lows start off in the mid 30s to lower 40s on Tuesday \nnight and only moderate slightly to the upper 30s to mid 40s by \nthe weekend. Lingering patchy frost is probable Tuesday night into\nearly Wednesday for the sheltered valleys, and additional isolated\npatchy frost cannot be entirely ruled out later in the week in \nthe coldest sheltered hollows. Otherwise, at least patchy fog is \nlikely along the mainstem rivers and larger tributaries on most \nnights.",
